# Basic syntax and the history store.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# The Tau REPL evaluates two kinds of expression: terms, which denote Boolean
# functions, and formulas, which denote Tau specifications. Every expression you
# type is stored in the history, so a result can be fed to a later command.

# The history works like an array; the first stored expression or result is at
# position 1. Positions can be given absolutely, from the beginning, as
# "%position", or relatively, from the end, as "%-offset". Plain "%" is the last
# result, and is the same as "%-0".

# Let us store a few terms by simply typing them:

(X | Y) & Z  # | is disjunction and & is conjunction
(X Y) | Z    # & may be omitted between two operands, so `X Y` means `X & Y`
(X ^ Y) & Z' # ^ is exclusive or, ' is complementation
(Y ^ 1) | 0  # 1 and 0 are the top and bottom elements of the Boolean algebra

# Note that ^ is the *term* level exclusive or. Formulas use ^^ instead, and +
# is not a Boolean operator at all -- it is bitvector addition, which only means
# something for the bv types (see demo_2.3-solver-bitvectors.tau).

# To recall a stored expression:

hist %   # the last stored expression...
hist %-1 # ...the one before it
hist %1  # the first stored expression

# Or print the whole store at once:

hist

# Formulas are built from terms by relating them. Equality and its negation are
# the most common:

X = 0
X != 0

# Terms are also ordered by inclusion, which gives the remaining relations.
# `X <= Y` says that X is below Y, and there is a chained interval form:

X < Y
X <= Y
X !<= Y      # prefixing a relation with ! negates it
X <= Y <= Z  # both X <= Y and Y <= Z

# Formulas compose with their own connectives, spelled with doubled symbols to
# keep them distinct from the term operators:

X = 0 && Y = 0 || Z = 0    # || is disjunction and && is conjunction
X = 0 ^^ Y = 0 ^^ !(Z = 0) # ^^ is exclusive or and ! is negation
(X = 0 -> Y = 0) <-> Z = 0 # -> is implication and <-> is equivalence
X = 0 ? Y = 0 : Z = 0      # ? : is the conditional operator

# Variables are quantified with all and ex, which build a formula:

all X ex Y X = 0 && Y = 0  # all and ex are the universal and existential quantifiers

# There are also term level quantifiers, fall and fex, which build a term
# rather than a formula: `fall x f` is the meet of f over all values of x and
# `fex x f` is its join. They are currently parsed and carried through
# normalization as atomic terms, but not yet evaluated.

fall x (x | y)
fex x (x & y)

# Constants from a specific Boolean algebra are written between braces with the
# type appended. Here is a constant of the sbf Boolean algebra, the
# Lindenbaum-Tarski algebra of propositional logic, whose elements are simple
# Boolean functions:

{X}:sbf & Y | Z

# A type can also be attached to a variable or to 0 and 1, which is the other
# way to pin down which Boolean algebra an expression lives in:

x:sbf | y:sbf = 1:sbf

# An expression with no type information is assumed to be over a countable
# atomless Boolean algebra. All such algebras are isomorphic, so no particular
# model is chosen.

# Specifications talk about time. Input and output stream variables are indexed
# by a time point: i... are inputs, o... are outputs, `t` is the current time
# point and `t-1` the previous one.

o1[t] = i1[t] || o2[t] != i2[t]
o1[t] = i1[t] && o2[t] = i2[t]
o1[t] = i1[t] && !(o2[t] = i2[t])

# A specification with no `always` or `sometimes` is implicitly an `always`
# statement, so the three formulas above are asserted at every time point. Both
# operators can be written out or given as the modal boxes:

always o1[t] = 0
[] o1[t] = 0        # the same thing
sometimes o1[t] = 0
<> o1[t] = 0        # the same thing

# A time point can also be constrained directly. A constant time constraint is
# written in brackets and compares the time variable against a number, which is
# how a specification says something about a bounded stretch of time. Below, the
# output is 1 for the first two steps and 0 from then on:

([t < 2] -> o1[t] = 1) && ([t >= 2] -> o1[t] = 0)

# Because Tau specifications are themselves elements of a Boolean algebra -- the
# Tau Boolean algebra -- a specification can appear as a constant inside another
# specification:

{o1[t] = i1[t] || o2[t] != i2[t]} & {o1[t] != 0}
{o1[t] = i1[t] && o2[t] = i2[t]} | {o1[t] != 0}
{o1[t] = i1[t] && !(o2[t] = i2[t])}

({ i1[t]'o1[t] = 0 } i1[t] = 0) ? (o1[t] = 0) : (o1[t] = 1)

# The type may be omitted on those constants because the Tau Boolean algebra is
# the default. This is what lets a Tau specification talk about Tau
# specifications, and the nesting repeats to any depth: a specification about
# specifications about specifications, and so on. demo_3.2 executes programs of
# exactly this shape.

# Finally, an uninterpreted constant stands for an unknown element of the
# algebra, behaving like a variable existentially quantified from outside the
# formula:

o1[t] = <:a>

quit
